      "hints": [
        "Integrate to get $y = 2x^{3/2} - 6x^{1/2} + c$.",
        "Substitute $(3, 5)$ and note $2(3^{3/2}) = 6\\sqrt{3}$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Integrate $\\frac{dy}{dx} = 3x^{1/2} - 3x^{-1/2}$ term-by-term:\n$$y = \\int \\left(3x^{1/2} - 3x^{-1/2}\\right)\\,dx = \\frac{3x^{3/2}}{\\frac{3}{2}} - \\frac{3x^{1/2}}{\\frac{1}{2}} + c = 2x^{3/2} - 6x^{1/2} + c$$",
        "Substitute the point $(3, 5)$ to determine the constant of integration $c$:\n$$5 = 2(3)^{3/2} - 6(3)^{1/2} + c = 2(3\\sqrt{3}) - 6\\sqrt{3} + c = 6\\sqrt{3} - 6\\sqrt{3} + c \\implies c = 5$$",
        "State the complete equation of the curve:\n$$y = 2x^{3/2} - 6x^{1/2} + 5$$"
      ],
      "checking": {
